    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/bg-7-57-2010",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Photosynthetic CO2 uptake by oceanic phytoplankton and subsequent export of particulate organic carbon (POC) to the ocean interior comprises a globally significant biological carbon pump, controlled in part by the composition of the planktonic community. The strength and efficiency of this pump depends upon the balance of particle production in the euphotic zone and remineralization of those particles in the mesopelagic (defined here as depths between 150 and 300 m), but how these processes respond to climate-driven changes in the physical environment is not completely understood. In the Sargasso Sea, from ~1996\u20132007, we have observed a decade-long &gt;50% increase in euphotic zone integrated autotrophic biomass (estimated from chlorophyll TChl-\u03b1), prokaryotic phytoplankton, primary production and shallow (150 m) POC export coinciding with a shift in the mean phase of the winter North Atlantic Oscillation (NAO) from consistently positive to neutral but variable. During this same period mesopelagic POC flux attenuation has doubled such that carbon sequestration below 300 m, the maximum winter/spring ventilation depth, has not changed. The increased mesopelagic POC attenuation appears mediated by changes in plankton community composition and metabolic activity in both the euphotic and mesopelagic zones. These changes are counter to extant hypotheses regarding inter-relationships between phytoplankton community composition, productivity and carbon export, and have significant impacts on how the Sargasso Sea ecosystem, at least, is modeled. Moreover, these time-series observations suggest that processes in the euphotic zone and mesopelagic are tightly coupled and should be considered together in future research.",
    "field": "Marine biogeochemistry",
    "problem": "Existing understanding of the ocean's biological carbon pump is incomplete, as it does not fully explain how climate-driven changes in surface production are tightly coupled with and counteracted by enhanced mid-ocean remineralization, which ultimately limits deep-ocean carbon sequestration.",
    "method": "This study analyzes a long-term biogeochemical time-series to correlate observed changes in primary production, carbon export, and mesopelagic attenuation in the Sargasso Sea with a documented shift in the North Atlantic Oscillation, thereby explaining the ecosystem's response to climate variability.",
    "task": "The task is to analyze long-term observational data from the Sargasso Sea to understand how climate variability affects the biological carbon pump, specifically the production, export, and subsequent attenuation of particulate organic carbon and its link to changes in the plankton community.",
    "model": [
        "NAO",
        "PDO"
    ],
